    {
      "key": "stiffness-scale",
      "usage": "bench",
      "description": "Assumed global log-uniform prior centred on six reference forces with the published between-patient width",
      "notes": "Assumed calibration of this solver, not a measurement of the child. On the six made-up spines, use each first proposed placement with no overbend, 6 mm titanium-alloy rods (selected Prasad modulus 114 GPa), the unchanged fit, stiffness scale 1 and the existing middle draw for every other correction unknown, including rib factors and the placement's release. Largest head forces after locking and rigid release, before springback, in N: right-thoracic-40 seeds 1/2 = 317.363815, 194.374529; double-major-55-to-45 seeds 1/2 = 543.417206, 320.981726; lumbar-left-30 seeds 1/2 = 160.260443, 200.155813. Divide the geometric mean of the published 192 and 439 N endpoints by the geometric mean of these six forces to obtain centre c = 1.096180421361. Lower = c / sqrt(439/192) = 0.724937215262; upper = c * sqrt(439/192) = 1.657538736979. Store the endpoints rounded to the nearest hundredth (two decimal places), halfway values up: 0.72 and 1.66. Before rounding, the upper/lower factor is exactly the published 439/192 = 2.286458333333. The width equals the spread of the largest force across the six published patients, as if all of it came from stiffness; six patients are few, and the same report's summed forces spread by a factor of 3.1, so the true spread could be wider. Salmingo et al. 2013, Clin Biomech, n = 6, PMID 23273729, author manuscript read in full: the largest screw force, 192 to 439 N across six patients, was worked back from rod deformation. The 6 mm titanium-alloy rod is recorded for the same group's earlier three-patient report (Salmingo 2012, PMID 22321374) and is assumed for the six. The reference forces are taken with the rods held rigid, before springback, so the rod chosen does not change them, while the published forces come from rods after they flexed. The six reference spines differ among themselves by a factor of 3.4 at scale 1, more than the published 2.29, so one spine's forces at the stored range can still pass 439 N. This rule assumes locking forces scale linearly with tissue stiffness; it does not fit springback or daily forces. Petit's different-baseline fitted factors remain beside it.",
      "values": [
        {
          "term": "lower",
          "value": 0.72,
          "unit": "ratio",
          "source": "project-assumptions",
          "section": "project assumption: stiffness scale centred on six reference cases with published between-patient width",
